A practical refactor plan (without breaking what’s working)
You’re at the “it works, now make it nice” stage—perfect time to separate concerns and make error-handling consistent.
1) Restructure into modules (biggest readability win)
Right now main.c mixes board bring-up, I2C low-level,
DMA, touch controller driver, and application logic. I’d split into:
bsp_i2c3.{c,h}
Owns: GPIO init for I2C3 pins, I2C3 timing init, bus recovery (ReleaseSerialBus), low-level read/write helpers, DMA read helper, timeouts.
stmpe811.{c,h}
Owns: register map, init sequence, interrupt config, read XY, clear interrupt/fifo, etc.
touch.{c,h} (optional)
Owns: “application policy” like Touch_Process(), debounce/filtering, stats, retry strategy.
Keep main.c as orchestration only (init + main loop).
This makes it much easier to optimize later without turning main.c into spaghetti again.
2) Standardize your “address + status + timeout” story
You currently have multiple “status styles”:
enum AddrError (good)
functions returning uint8_t but using negative values (not good)
multiple timeouts with magic numbers
Refactor goal:
use one status type (enum or int8_t) across driver layer
use named constants for timeouts
use one convention for I2C addresses (7-bit vs 8-bit)
3) Keep ISRs minimal and deterministic
Your DMA IRQ handler currently does a lot (including I2C transactions). Eventually you’ll want:
ISR: clear flags, stop DMA/I2C safely, set a completion/error flag
main loop: do sensor “cleanup” work (fifo reset, re-enable interrupts, etc.)
That reduces ISR latency and makes race conditions easier to reason about.

One high-impact fix to do immediately: stop returning negative values from uint8_t
In your current code, STMPE811_Write_Reg_Safe() is declared uint8_t but returns -1, -2. That wraps to 255, 254 and makes error handling confusing/bug-prone.
Below are minimal edits to make the return type consistent (int8_t) and update the related prototypes.
